    "biography": "<p>Philippine vampires (aswang) that eat internal organs instead of drinking blood.</p><p><strong>Powers:</strong> Enhanced Physical Characteristics, Enhanced Senses (Hearing and Smell), Essence Drain, Immunity (Age, Poison and Pathogens), Infection, Mist Form, Regeneration, Thermographic Vision</p><p><strong>Weaknesses:</strong> Allergy (Sunlight, Severe), Induced Dormancy (lack of air), Essence Loss, Vulnerability (Salt, Wood), in some a psychologically based Allergy (Holy Objects, Severe)</p><p><strong>Note:</strong> Every vampire was a character first and keeps most of that character's attributes (the book's \"C\"): Body, Charisma, Intelligence, Willpower and Reaction are the character's; Quickness is the character's with a running multiplier of 5; Strength is the character's Strength plus the vampire's Essence; Essence is 2D6. This sheet is a typical vampire built from an average human (every attribute 3) with Essence 7, so Strength 10. To make a particular vampire, use the original character's attributes, roll 2D6 for Essence and set Strength to Strength + Essence. Essence Drain raises Essence up to twice its type's maximum — 12 for a humanoid (core p.217).</p><p><em>Printed stats: B C · Q C × 5 · S C + E · C C · I C · W C · E 2D6 · R C; Attacks Humanoid. Running multiplier ×5. Cyberpirates p.149.</em></p><p><em>On the sheet: the sheet's running rate uses ×3 — use ×5 by hand; Essence 7 and Strength 10 are a typical vampire's — roll 2D6 for Essence and use the original character's attributes; Threat Rating 4 is this module's estimate (the average attribute), not the book's.</em></p>",
